## Cost

The average cost of breast augmentation surgery alone is **$4,516**, according to 2020 statistics from the American Society of Plastic Surgeons: however depending on factors such as implant size and material, office location, and your doctor’s experience total prices can range from **$6,000** to **$12,000** (this includes fees, facility costs, and post-procedure treatments.)

Since breast augmentation can be *quite* expensive I personally would recommend holding off on this until you have completed the previous steps talked about in this guide. By doing this, you give yourself some time to **explore what being a bimbo means to you** and what you want to look like before making any permanent changes. Also, this will give you some time to **save money** before you sign up for an expensive procedure.

## Options

### Saline

Saline is only available in the US (everywhere else in the world uses silicone) and is a sterile saltwater solution. This type of implant is a popular choice because A) **If your implant ruptures, it won’t take you long to notice** since your body will begin to absorb the saline and B) **Saline implants also leave shorter incisions**, usually about one inch, while silicone incisions are usually about one and a half to two inches. It’s also **easier to achieve the super fake pornstar** look with saline implants if that’s what you want, although it is possible to achieve the same look with silicone. Saline is known for being harder than silicone, but many women have saline implants that are not firm or solid-feeling at all, so your results may vary. One

--- Page 11 ---

possible issue with saline is an increased chance of rippling. This is more likely to occur in thin women who aren't starting with a lot of breast tissue. One way to reduce rippling in saline implants is by filling them to the max volume.

### Silicone

Silicone implants are generally **softer** and many surgeons prefer to use them on thinner women and women who don't have a lot of breast tissue to start with. These implants are a highly cohesive gel that is very unlikely to leak into the rest of your body if they rupture. Many women *don't even know* their silicone implants have ruptured unless they have another surgery, and it's often discovered when removing the old ones. If you're concerned about having silicone in your body know that A) Silicone is FDA recommended for **any woman over 22** and B) Even if you get saline implants the shell containing the saline is **made of silicone**.

### Negative Side-Effects

### Rupture

No matter what kind of implants you get, **the truth is that there will always be a chance of a rupture occurring**. Your doctor will go over the specifics with you as you speak with them, but the FDA recommends getting an MRI to check for ruptures 3 years after your surgery and every 2 years after that. Note that a rupture will likely not require an MRI to detect if you have saline implants.

